<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niccolò Paganini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1782–1840</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Niccolò Paganini was an Italian violinist and composer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, the most celebrated virtuoso of his age. From 1805 to 1809 he served at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa Bonaparte</a> in Lucca, before touring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and, from 1828, the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where his technique and stage presence made him a legend. His 24 Caprices for solo violin remain central to the instrument's repertoire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niccolo-paganini.jpg" alt="Niccolò Paganini" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Whittle (active in Preston, early 19th century) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paganini.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
